#!/usr/bin/env python3
# =============================================================================
# batch_forecast_comparison.py
# Standalone (no Streamlit / no UI) batch forecaster for GLC Sales Order data
# =============================================================================
#
# WHAT THIS IS
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   A separate, headless companion to production_forecasting_mvp.py. It does
#   NOT import streamlit and has no interface. It reads the same Sales Order
#   workbook, forecasts the next 21 days of daily Net Pounds for:
#     - every (customer, material group) combination found in the data, and
#     - the overall (all-customer, all-material) total
#   using 4 models (Seasonal Naive, AutoARIMA, AutoETS,
#   HistGradientBoostingRegressor -- Chronos-2 is intentionally left out of
#   this batch script; it needs a GPU/optional install and is far slower per
#   segment, which doesn't fit an 800+-segment weekly batch job), plus the
#   straight average of those 4 numbers for each day.
#
#   With 800+ customer/material combinations, re-running all 4 models live
#   every time someone wants to look at one combination is too slow and not
#   necessary. Instead this script runs ONCE (e.g. weekly), and:
#     - writes every segment's backtest accuracy + 21-day forecast into one
#       SQLite file (fast, indexed, compact -- no re-running models to read
#       a result), and
#     - additionally writes the OVERALL forecast to a small Excel file,
#       since that's the one you're comparing directly against your own
#       existing Excel predictions.
#   Reading results back afterward (for one customer/material, or all of
#   them) is just a fast filtered SQL query -- see read_segment_forecast()
#   / read_segment_metrics() / list_segments() near the bottom.
#
# INSTALLATION
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   pip install pandas numpy openpyxl scikit-learn statsforecast scipy
#   (same core dependencies as production_forecasting_mvp.py, minus
#   streamlit/plotly -- this script never renders anything)
#
# RUN
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   python batch_forecast_comparison.py --workbook "Sales Order 2024-26.xlsx"
#
#   Useful flags (all optional, see --help for the full list):
#     --db forecasts.sqlite          where results are written/read (default)
#     --excel-out overall_forecast.xlsx   the overall-forecast Excel file
#     --horizon 21                   forecast horizon in days
#     --n-windows 8                  preferred backtest windows per segment
#     --thorough-arima               use full AutoARIMA search instead of the
#                                     faster reduced-search default (slower,
#                                     modestly more accurate)
#     --n-jobs -1                    CPU cores to use (-1 = all available)
#     --limit 50                     only process the first N segments
#                                     (useful for a quick timing test before
#                                     committing to a full 800+-segment run)
#     --overwrite                    recompute segments even if this run_date
#                                     already has results for them
#
# WHY THIS RUNTIME BUDGET MATTERS
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   AutoARIMA is, by a wide margin, the slowest of the 4 models per segment.
#   Benchmarked on this development machine (single CPU core): a full
#   8-window backtest of one ~1000-day segment took ~15-30s with AutoARIMA's
#   default search settings, vs. ~3-6s with approximation=True and a
#   restricted max_order -- roughly a 5-6x speedup for a modest accuracy
#   tradeoff. That's the difference between several hours and well under an
#   hour across 800+ segments once spread over your actual CPU core count
#   via --n-jobs. The faster settings are the default here; use
#   --thorough-arima if you'd rather have the slower, more exhaustive search.
#   Run with --limit 30 first to get a real per-segment timing on your own
#   hardware before committing to a full run.
#
# =============================================================================

